  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Max begins the morning convinced that nothing can ruin his wonderful mood. However, an encounter with Dmitry Nagiyev at the restaurant entrance and sudden news from his best friend Kostya quickly turn his day upside down. Listen to the Russian conversation first, and then we will unpack each line.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "На сегодня мой прогноз отличный.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хочется войти через парадный вход.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ничто не испортит мне настроение.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Бывает, что прогнозы врут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Иди на задний двор.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Не позорь наш ресторан.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это не лучший мой день.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Друзья всегда меня поддержат.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Здорово, Макс, тут такое дело.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Тебе нужно съехать с квартиры.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "У нас малыш скоро родится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Скорее бы этот день закончился.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "На сегодня мой прогноз отличный.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'My forecast for today is excellent.' Max anchors the line on 'on', while 'excellent' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хочется войти через парадный вход.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I feel like walking in through the front entrance.' The meaning hinges on 'want' working together with 'entrance' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ничто не испортит мне настроение.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Nothing will ruin my mood.' The negative particle sits right before the verb meaning 'to spoil' to negate the statement.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Бывает, что прогнозы врут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Sometimes forecasts lie.' In Russian, the idea of 'it is' flows naturally into 'they lie' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Иди на задний двор.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Go to the back courtyard.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'go' and finishing on 'yard'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Не позорь наш ресторан.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do not disgrace our restaurant.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'not' and finishing on 'restaurant'. Max answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это не лучший мой день.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is not my best day.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'this' before landing on 'day'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Друзья всегда меня поддержат.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Friends will always support me.' Max brings out the contrast between 'friend' and 'always' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Здорово, Макс, тут такое дело.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hey, Max, here is the thing.' Kostya anchors the line on 'great', while 'business' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Тебе нужно съехать с квартиры.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You need to move out of the apartment.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'to you' before landing on 'apartments'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "У нас малыш скоро родится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Our baby will be born soon.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'at' and finishing on 'will be born'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Скорее бы этот день закончился.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I wish this day would end soon.' In Russian, the idea of 'quickly' flows naturally into 'to end' without extra filler words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
